oot Cause: If the identity isn't registered, the server is likely failing the authentication or authorization check. In gRPC-web, this doesn't usually result in a 401/403 HTTP error; instead, the server returns a 200 OK but sets the grpc-status header to 16 (Unauthenticated) or 7 (Permission Denied), then immediately closes the stream.\n\nSuggestions:\n\nVerify grpc-status: Check the response headers for grpc-status. If it is 16, your identity/token is missing or invalid; if 7, the identity is known but lacks permissions.\nCheck Request Headers: Ensure the x-ghostbridge-footprint or other metadata headers intended to carry your identity are correctly populated and not empty.\nDebug Auth Middleware: If you control the backend, check the interceptors/middleware for the Subscribe method to see if they are rejecting the call before it reaches the service logic.\n\n\n\n\n\nCopy to coding agent
